    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\) in degrees, we will apply the concept of supplementary angles and linear pair properties.\n\nGiven:\n- \\(\\angle A = 93^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\angle B = 130^\\circ\\)\n\nWe know from the geometry of the provided diagram that:\n- Angle \\(\\angle B\\) and Angle \\(\\angle C\\) form a linear pair.\n- Angle \\(\\angle A\\) and Angle \\(\\angle C\\) also form a linear pair.\n\n1. **Understanding Linear Pairs**:\n    - A linear pair consists of two adjacent angles whose measures add up to \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\n2. **Applying Linear Pairs to Given Angles**:\n    - Since \\(\\angle B\\) and \\(\\angle C\\) are a linear pair:\n    \\[\n    \\angle B + \\angle C = 180^\\circ\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    130^\\circ + \\angle C = 180^\\circ\n    \\]\n\n3. **Solving for \\(\\angle C\\)**:\n    \\[\n    \\angle C = 180^\\circ - 130^\\circ\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    \\angle C = 50^\\circ\n    \\]\n\n4. **Revisiting \\(\\angle A\\)**:\n    - Note that \\(\\angle A\\) and \\(\\angle C\\) are part of the same linear pair:\n    \\[\n    \\angle A + \\angle C = 180^\\circ\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    93^\\circ + 50^\\circ = 143^\\circ\n    \\]\n\n5. **Reconsidering Linear Pairs with \\(\\angle A\\)**:\n    - We realize \\(\\angle A\\) forms a linear pair with the information about \\(\\angle B\\) and \\(\\angle C\\).\n\n6. **Correct Calculation for \\(\\angle ACB\\)**:\n    - Determine \\(\\angle ACB\\) by considering the remaining angle from the straight line formation:\n    \\[\n    \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ - \\angle A\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ - 50^\\circ\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    \\angle ACB = 130^\\circ\n    \\]\n\nThus, the measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\) is \\(\\boxed{130^\\circ}\\).",
